Objective:
Major central concepts/issues such as equivalence, loss and gain (in translation), translatability/untranslatability, and visibility/invisibility will be examined in the course. By the end of the course, students are expected to:
become familiar with various theories of translation in the course of history and be able to define central concepts found in translation theories and/or Translation Studies;
be able to describe the nature of (human) translation and AI-generated translation;
to apply theory to explain why certain translations are made in certain ways.

Upon semester completion, students will be able to:
communicate in English with greater confidence and fluency in everyday and classroom situations;
participate actively and appropriately in conversations, discussions, and collaborative tasks;
express and organize ideas, opinions, and personal experiences clearly and coherently;
adjust their language and communication style according to different audiences, purposes, and levels of formality; and
comprehend a variety of spoken texts and deliver short prepared and impromptu presentations effectively.

