課程簡介:
哈佛大學前校長康納特(
James Bryant Conant
)說：如果我們將學過的東西忘得一乾二淨，最後剩下來的就是教育的本質。李開復也認為：大學的學習目的，最主要是掌握基本的學習工具和方法，將來利用這些工具和方法，再去學習新事物。因此，與其說上大學是為了學一門專業，不如說是為了學會如何學習，讓自己能夠無師自通。基於上述理念，本課程特別強調學習方法論的建立，使學習者不僅習得一門法律學科的技術性知識，更重要的是得以學習自我學習的方法。準此，本課程主要介紹民事訴訟法的基本概念、體系架構及實務運作，融合理論分析與實務現況，使學習者認識我國現行民事訴訟的流程及解決具體問題的方式。

課程目標與學習成效:
The course aims to build up students’ communicative and grammatic competence in German at a beginner’s level (corresponding approximately to level A1 of the Common European Framework of Reference for Languages). Students will have the chance to read easy texts in German, practice in basic listening comprehension, writing, and speaking skills through a variety of interactive exercises. In addition, the lecturer aims to show the students the structure and requirements of German official language certificate exam for level A1 (conducted by
Goethe Institut
). Pronunciation and basic sentence structure are the two main elements in this course, to introduce the students to the – compared to Mandarin – quite complex grammatic structure. Students shall be able and willing to communicate in German on a simple but efficient level.

By the end of the course, students should be able to
1. properly define the basic concepts of propbability space and state well;
2. understand the definitions and properties of conditional probability and independence;
3. demonstrate ability to campute and determine various expectations of functions of random variables;
4. show the capability of calculating probabilities of events for a probability model and relating these to real-world applications;
5. identify distributions of special random variables;
6. underdtand the laws of large numbers and be able to apply them to solve the problems;
7. underdtand the central limit thoerem and be able to apply it to solve the problems.

課程簡介:
This course is designed to teach students the following points:
to provide an overview of Web Search and Mining related research;
to systematically review the core research topics in the field;
to show case the most recent research progress;
to give students enough training for doing research in the field and an opportunity to work on a research project.

課程目標與學習成效:
This interdisciplinary course explores contemporary challenges to democracy in Asia in the post-Cold War period through comparative case studies.
It situates these challenges within broader global transformations, including the Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs), technological change, and the growing political significance of digital infrastructures and artificial intelligence (AI) in shaping governance and state-society relations in Southeast Asia.
The course is divided into two parts: theoretical foundations and country studies. It introduces key analytical concepts, tools, and methodologies that enable students to assess regime types and evaluate pathways for strengthening democratic governance in East and Southeast Asia. This approach provides students with a deeper understanding of both structural and institutional constraints on democratization, as well as the evolving role of technology in political contestation and state capacity.
Upon completing the course, students will be able to:
(1) understand distinctions between regime types and accurately identify political typologies across East and Southeast Asia;
(2) analyze democratization trajectories and explain patterns of democratic consolidation and backsliding in selected cases, including Myanmar, Indonesia, and to some extent Malaysia;
(3) identify country-specific structural, institutional, and socio-political impediments to democratization,
including the role of digital technologies such as social media platforms, algorithmic governance, disinformation ecosystems, and state regulation of online expression
;
(4) evaluate how historical trajectories of nationalism, modernization, and technological change interact to shape contemporary patterns of democratic development and authoritarian resilience in the region.

